**Theorem 2.3 (The consumed preparation-response condition).** Fix finitely
many real unit measurement seeds $b_f$ and weights $w_f>0$ with
$\sum_fw_f\int_{\mathcal G}gb_fb_f^Tg^T\,dg=I$.
For real density matrices $\sigma_f$, define

$$
S_\sigma(X)=\sum_fw_f\int_{\mathcal G}
g\sigma_fg^T\operatorname{tr}(gb_fb_f^Tg^TX)\,dg.
\tag{2.21}
$$

Suppose the maximum (1.8) is attained only at one signed event
$sH_{J_*}(S_\sigma)$, $s\in\{1,-1\}$, whose largest eigenvalue
$z>0$ is simple, with real unit eigenvector $v$. Define

$$
C_f=s\int_{\mathcal G}
\operatorname{tr}(vv^Tgb_fb_f^Tg^T)\,g^T\Pi_{J_*}g\,dg.
\tag{2.22}
$$

A local minimum over these preparations with these measurement seeds
fixed must obey

$$
\operatorname{tr}(\sigma_f C_f)=\lambda_{\max}(C_f),\qquad
\operatorname{ran}\sigma_f\subseteq
\ker(\lambda_{\max}(C_f)I-C_f)
\quad\text{for every }f.
\tag{2.23}
$$

If a real density $\tau_f$ instead satisfies
$\operatorname{tr}[(\tau_f-\sigma_f)C_f]>0$, the corresponding
preparation mixture strictly decreases the original error for every
sufficiently small positive step.

Proof. Each channel in (2.21) has the same full input marginal,
because only the preparation trace enters TP. It is an admissible
finite fixed scalar port: decompose a real density into real pure
states, and use QRC Theorem 5.2's exact $50$-point torus/reflection
quadrature. Alternatively keep the rotated mixed density as decoder
at each of the $50$ fixed measurement flags per seed. The coefficients
have total angular degree at most four, so this latter choice also
gives exactly (2.21), with an encoder independent of $\sigma$.

Replacing one $\sigma_f$ by $(1-t)\sigma_f+t\tau_f$ changes the
active signed eigenvalue at derivative
$-w_f\operatorname{tr}[(\tau_f-\sigma_f)C_f]$. For completeness,
the derivative of a simple top eigenvalue of $M+tN$ is $v^TNv$:
the Rayleigh expressions at $v$ and at a maximizing $v_t$ sandwich
the difference quotient, and simplicity forces
$v_tv_t^T\to vv^T$. All other signed event eigenvalues have a strict
gap; finiteness and continuity preserve that gap for sufficiently
small $t$. A negative derivative therefore decreases the full maximum.

The fixed-measurement spectral maximization used here is the standard
ingredient of [Fuchs–Sasaki, §3, equations (13)–(16)](https://arxiv.org/html/quant-ph/0302092v3):
maximizing a linear preparation payoff over density matrices gives its
largest eigenvalue. Their ensemble-fidelity objective is not (1.3);
the signed-event derivative and finite inactive-event gap above provide
the needed connection here. Zero trace pairing of the two PSD matrices
$\sigma_f$ and $\lambda_{\max}(C_f)I-C_f$ is equivalent to the
range condition in (2.23). A violation thus gives the asserted descent.
The statement makes no claim with multiple active signed events or a
repeated active eigenvalue. $\square$

